mothershipOS: size the device's buffer as one guest period (SET_PARAMS period_bytes), in two device periods,
instead of a fixed ~100 ms period and 2 of them. The backend completes a guest buffer as soon as it is copied into
the device, so the device buffer is how far ahead of real time it takes the guest's audio. With 200 ms (the
PipeWire ALSA plugin grants it; the DisplayPort dmix capped it lower) it completed the guest's whole buffer at
every start; with two guest periods it still took everything Android prefills: either way the guest underran one
period after each start, stopped, prepared and started again, endlessly (choppy audio). One period ahead is safe.

--- a/src/audio_backends/alsa.rs
+++ b/src/audio_backends/alsa.rs
@@ -176,7 +176,12 @@
 
             // Calculate period size for ~100ms (arbitrary) interrupt period:
 
-            let period_bytes = bps_rate / 10;
+            // mothershipOS: half the guest's period (its SET_PARAMS; ~100 ms only if it gave none), the device buffer
+            // two of them: the host runs at most one guest period ahead of real time
+            let period_bytes = match u32::from(s.params.period_bytes) {
+                0 => bps_rate / 10,
+                b => b / 2,
+            };
 
             // Finally, calculate the size of a period (in frames):
 
@@ -195,8 +200,9 @@
             // > as well as the parameters set in the snd_pcm_hardware structure (in the driver).
             //
             // So, if the operation fails let's assume the ALSA runtime has set a better value.
-            if let Err(err) = hwp.set_buffer_size_near(2 * i64::from(period_frames)) {
-                log::error!("could not set buffer size {}: {}", 2 * period_frames, err);
+            let buffer_frames = 2 * period_frames;
+            if let Err(err) = hwp.set_buffer_size_near(i64::from(buffer_frames)) {
+                log::error!("could not set buffer size {}: {}", buffer_frames, err);
             }
 
             // Read more at https://www.alsa-project.org/wiki/FramesPeriods.
